meta {
  name: 02 Create payment
  type: http
  seq: 2
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/payments
  body: json
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
  Idempotency-Key: {{idempotencyKey}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "seats": 10
  }
}

script:post-response {
  if (res.getStatus() === 201) {
    bru.setEnvVar("paymentId", res.getBody().id);
  }
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 201
  res.body.status: eq PENDING
  res.body.amountXaf: eq 48000
}

docs {
  Records an intent to pay. NO MONEY MOVES, and nothing is granted.

  Check `Center Subscription / 01 Get subscription` before and after: `paidUntil`
  is unchanged and `seats` is unchanged. Only a verified Notch Pay event may
  move those, and that arrives in Phase 7. `status` stays PENDING because
  nothing in this phase can advance it.

  ## Set the key first

  Put any unique value in `{{idempotencyKey}}` in the environment — a uuid is
  the intended shape. Change it whenever you want a genuinely NEW payment.

  ## The three things to try, in this order

  1. SEND IT TWICE WITHOUT CHANGING ANYTHING.
     Both return 201, and `id` is the SAME both times. A double-clicked button
     is one intent, not two payments. Confirm with `03 Payment history`: one
     row, not two.

  2. SAME KEY, DIFFERENT SEATS. Change the body to `{"seats": 20}` and resend
     with the same key -> 409 IDEMPOTENCY_KEY_REUSED.
     Handing back the original would tell you that you had bought 20 seats
     while the record says 10, and that disagreement would only surface when
     reconciling against the provider.

  3. NEW KEY, SAME SEATS -> a genuinely new payment with a new id. The key is
     what identifies the attempt, not the body.

  ## Without a key

  Remove the `Idempotency-Key` header -> 400 IDEMPOTENCY_KEY_REQUIRED.

  It is required rather than generated server-side on purpose: a key the
  server invents makes every retry a fresh payment, which is precisely the
  failure idempotency exists to prevent. Empty and whitespace-only keys are
  refused for the same reason.

  ## The price is not an input, here either

  All of these -> 400:

  - `{"seats": 10, "unitPriceXaf": 1}`
  - `{"seats": 10, "amountXaf": 1}`
  - `{"seats": 10, "status": "SUCCEEDED"}`

  That last one matters most. Without it a client could ask for a payment that
  claims to have already succeeded.

  ## While BLOCKED

  This works. It is the point of the whole road-out design: a lapsed center
  must be able to pay. If this ever returns 403, someone has added
  CenterSubscriptionGuard to the payments controller —
  `test/center-blocked-surface.spec.ts` should have failed first.
}
